A practical pilot could be AI-assisted customer support ticket handling. When a ticket arrives, the AI can classify it by issue type, summarize the customer's problem, identify relevant information from the knowledge base, and draft a response. A human reviews and approves the response before sending it.
We can measure the pilot against the current process using response time, resolution time, accuracy, and percentage of AI drafts requiring major edits. Start with a limited set of low-risk tickets, review the results weekly, and refine the prompts and knowledge base based on feedback. This gives us a manageable test with clear, measurable business impact.
We can standardize prompts by creating reusable templates for common tasks, with clear instructions, expected outputs, constraints, and examples. For company context, we should provide the AI with relevant information such as our processes, policies, terminology, product details, and approved documentation rather than relying on generic knowledge.
We should also maintain a central prompt library, version-control important prompts, and test them against representative scenarios. For sensitive or high-impact tasks, the output should still go through human review. This gives teams a consistent way to use AI while allowing us to continuously improve prompts based on actual results.
Yes. I think we should provide short, practical training rather than making it overly technical. It could cover AI fundamentals, effective prompting, how to provide the right company context, and the limitations of AI-generated outputs.
We should also establish clear guidelines around data privacy, approved AI tools, acceptable use cases, and when human review is required. A few hands-on examples using our actual workflows would help employees understand the approach and build confidence.
Finally, we can provide a simple reference guide with approved prompt templates, best practices, and examples so people have something they can use after the training.
Before rolling it out, I think we should start with a small pilot group and collect feedback on the training, prompt templates, and workflows. We can use that feedback to refine the guidelines before expanding to the wider team.
We should also define clear success metrics, such as time saved, output quality, adoption, and error rates. Alongside that, we should have a process for reporting issues and updating prompts or guidelines as our use of AI evolves.
That would give us a controlled way to launch, measure the impact, and improve the approach over time.
The agent should have a clearly defined role, goals, and constraints. Its role should specify what it is responsible for, while its goals should describe the desired outcomes. Constraints must clearly state what the agent is not allowed to do. For example, the agent can analyze requests, provide recommendations, and prepare actions, but it should not send messages, modify records, make purchases, or perform irreversible actions without approval. Define which actions require human confirmation and when the agent must escalate to a person. This structure helps prevent the agent from interpreting broad objectives as permission to take unexpected or unauthorized actions.
